WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.353 --> 00:00:02.604
This video is about the assistant.

2
00:00:02.604 --> 00:00:06.241
It's a chat panel that answers questions about your portfolio,

3
00:00:06.241 --> 00:00:09.585
and can carry out tasks for you, like cancelling orders.

4
00:00:09.585 --> 00:00:12.896
It runs on Claude, Anthropic's AI model.

5
00:00:13.563 --> 00:00:17.915
The assistant is off until you give it your own Anthropic API key.

6
00:00:17.915 --> 00:00:21.119
You add that on your profile, under the Assistant tab.

7
00:00:21.773 --> 00:00:27.042
Create a key in the Anthropic console, paste it here, and click Verify and Save.

8
00:00:27.042 --> 00:00:31.630
Cyrus checks it with Anthropic, then encrypts it like your exchange keys.

9
00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:37.162
Use is billed to your own Anthropic account, typically a fraction of a cent per question.

10
00:00:37.833 --> 00:00:41.073
One thing to know first: this is the one feature in

11
00:00:41.073 --> 00:00:44.312
Cyrus that sends portfolio data off your computer.

12
00:00:44.312 --> 00:00:49.733
When you ask a question, your holdings, rules, recent activity and resting orders

13
00:00:49.733 --> 00:00:54.685
go straight to Anthropic under your key, with no Cyrus server in between.

14
00:00:55.364 --> 00:00:57.761
Your exchange keys are never part of that.

15
00:00:57.761 --> 00:01:02.987
This card sums up what to expect, and Remove key switches the assistant off again.

16
00:01:03.648 --> 00:01:05.845
Let's go to Limit Orders and open it.

17
00:01:05.845 --> 00:01:10.752
Click Ask in the top bar, or press Control and K on any page.

18
00:01:11.438 --> 00:01:14.340
The panel slides in over whatever page you're on.

19
00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.212
Under its title is the Claude model it uses.

20
00:01:17.876 --> 00:01:20.938
An empty conversation offers a few ideas.

21
00:01:20.938 --> 00:01:23.877
Click one, or type your own question at the bottom.

22
00:01:24.548 --> 00:01:26.838
Let's ask what the biggest positions are.

23
00:01:27.821 --> 00:01:31.795
It leads with the answer, then the detail: Bitcoin is about

24
00:01:31.795 --> 00:01:35.298
a third of the portfolio, then Ethereum and Solana.

25
00:01:35.298 --> 00:01:37.798
Demo replies are prepared in advance.

26
00:01:37.798 --> 00:01:41.056
With your key, Claude answers from your real accounts.

27
00:01:41.724 --> 00:01:46.758
It knows what you hold on every exchange and what it's worth, this month's change,

28
00:01:46.758 --> 00:01:51.302
your automation rules and their recent activity, and your resting orders.

29
00:01:51.302 --> 00:01:55.637
So you can ask how the month has gone, or have it explain your automations.

30
00:01:56.299 --> 00:02:00.664
The first question after opening the panel fetches fresh balances.

31
00:02:00.664 --> 00:02:04.294
Follow-ups within a minute reuse them, so they're quicker.

32
00:02:04.964 --> 00:02:09.435
It won't tell you what to buy or sell; it lays out the facts instead.

33
00:02:09.435 --> 00:02:14.608
And like any AI, it can be wrong, so double-check anything that matters.

34
00:02:15.268 --> 00:02:17.027
It can also do things for you.

35
00:02:17.027 --> 00:02:21.215
Let's ask it to cancel the Bitcoin sell orders priced in dollars.

36
00:02:21.884 --> 00:02:26.355
Before acting, it looks up your orders and picks the ones that match.

37
00:02:26.355 --> 00:02:30.558
Here it found two, on Kraken and Robinhood, and left a Bitcoin

38
00:02:30.558 --> 00:02:34.693
to Tether order alone, because that isn't priced in dollars.

39
00:02:35.372 --> 00:02:36.519
Then it stops.

40
00:02:36.519 --> 00:02:37.968
Nothing has happened yet.

41
00:02:37.968 --> 00:02:42.180
This card lists exactly what it's about to do, built from the real

42
00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:46.200
values Cyrus will send, not from the AI's description of them.

43
00:02:46.867 --> 00:02:49.570
Cancel turns it down, and nothing runs.

44
00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:52.282
Do it carries out every action on the card.

45
00:02:52.282 --> 00:02:55.700
If you ask something else instead, the card is dropped.

46
00:02:56.383 --> 00:02:59.830
Limit orders and automation rules work the same way, and

47
00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:03.155
limit orders and withdraw rules are flagged in amber.

48
00:03:03.155 --> 00:03:09.245
Read rules twice: a rule fires later, on its own, perhaps while you're asleep.

49
00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:13.607
Everything goes through the same checks as the buttons in Cyrus.

50
00:03:13.607 --> 00:03:16.853
So it can't place a market order, and can only withdraw

51
00:03:16.853 --> 00:03:19.923
to an address you've already whitelisted on Kraken.

52
00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:21.896
Let's approve this one.

53
00:03:23.812 --> 00:03:26.169
Each action reports back on its own.

54
00:03:26.169 --> 00:03:28.257
The Kraken order was cancelled.

55
00:03:28.257 --> 00:03:31.374
The Robinhood one had already filled, so the reason

56
00:03:31.374 --> 00:03:34.613
is shown right there, and the assistant explains it.

57
00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:37.594
Conversations are saved as you go.

58
00:03:37.594 --> 00:03:39.735
The clock button shows your history.

59
00:03:40.413 --> 00:03:44.085
History stays on this computer, separately for each account,

60
00:03:44.085 --> 00:03:46.839
and is never written to your Cyrus database.

61
00:03:46.839 --> 00:03:50.948
Click one to pick it up again, or delete one, or clear them all.

62
00:03:51.628 --> 00:03:54.610
The pencil button starts a new conversation.

63
00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:56.764
The old one stays in your history.

64
00:03:57.438 --> 00:04:00.885
Press Escape, or the cross, to close the panel.

65
00:04:00.885 --> 00:04:06.816
That's the assistant: ask in plain English, read the card, and approve only what you mean.

